<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">17 January in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">9 events that happened on 17 January, from 1377 to 1995.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-xi" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-xi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1377">1377 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">649 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Pope Gregory XI returns the papacy to Rome</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Urged on by Catherine of Siena, Gregory XI entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, ending almost seven decades in which the popes had lived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a>. His death the next year set off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-schism">Great Western Schism</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1773">1773 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">253 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">James Cook makes the first recorded crossing of the Antarctic Circle</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">HMS Resolution and HMS Adventure sailed south of 66°33' S, the first ships known to do so. Cook's voyage showed that any southern continent must lie far to the south, beyond the pack ice.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1781">1781 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">245 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Daniel Morgan routs Tarleton at the Battle of Cowpens</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a>, Daniel Morgan's Continentals and militia destroyed Banastre Tarleton's British force with a skilful double envelopment. The victory was a turning point in the southern campaign.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1893">1893 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">133 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Hawaii's Queen Liliuokalani overthrown</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">A committee of American and European businessmen, supported by US Marines from the USS <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>, deposed the Hawaiian queen and set up a provisional government. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> in 1898.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1945">1945 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">81 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Soviet and Polish troops enter the ruins of Warsaw</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> and the Polish First Army took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, abandoned by the Germans. After the crushing of the 1944 Uprising, the Germans had systematically razed the city, destroying about 85 per cent of it.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1946">1946 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">80 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">UN Security Council meets for the first time</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-security-council">United Nations Security Council</a> held its first session at Church House, Westminster. Its five permanent members, each with a veto, were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1961">1961 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">65 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Eisenhower warns of the 'military-industrial complex'</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">In his televised farewell address, the outgoing President cautioned that a permanent arms industry and huge defence establishment could acquire 'unwarranted influence' over American government.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1991">1991 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">35 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Operation Desert Storm opens with air strikes on Iraq</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">A US-led coalition began a massive air campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, which had occupied Kuwait since August 1990. A ground offensive in late February expelled Iraqi forces within 100 hours.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1995">1995 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">31 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Great Hanshin earthquake devastates Kobe</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">A magnitude 6.9 earthquake struck the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kobe">Kobe</a> region before dawn, killing 6,434 people and destroying over 100,000 buildings. The slow official response prompted reform of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s disaster planning.</p></div></li></ol>
